It's probably not as simple as some of the other suggestions,
but I think even Windows has a telnet server that you can
activate.  You then implement the telnet protocol in your
program.  (A telnet client isn't too difficult, as protocols
go.)  And of course, if all he's interested in is running a
stock standard nethack, just opening a socket to a nethack
server would work.  (Now that would be wierd: easier to access
the program on a remote machine than it is locally.)

I believe that Expect (mentionned by Janis) is also available
for Windows.  That would probably be an easier choice.  If the
final program is in Lisp or some such, then Expect should be
able to communicate with it as well, at least if it can open a
socket.

> > while 1:
> >    get screen output from nethack

> It turns out this step has a lot of special cases. Examples include:
>  o prompting when you start nethack (to select a character, etc.)
>  o current cursor position is on the "@" [a somewhat reliable
> indicator that nethack is waiting for input but not always]; invisible
> players without "see invisible" can have a problem with this step
>  o -- More -- processing
> Properly parsing ANSI terminal codes (most likely for painting the
> screen) is an additional complication. I believe Rog-O-Matic
> (described below) redefined the terminal control codes to a much
> simpler set.

Parsing the ANSI sequences isn't that difficult, maintaining a
24x80 array, with the screen image.  Interpreting that image, of
course, is part of the challenge.  (As you say, the "image" must
be interpreted differently in different contexts.  On the other
hand, if he never saves, he might be able to get by with a
canned sequence of responses until the first level appears.)

